Database

mysql -u root -p //12345678

Step 1: See what exists
At the mysql> prompt, run:
sql
SHOW DATABASES;
You'll see a few built-in ones (information_schema, mysql, performance_schema, sys). Those belong to MySQL itself, so leave them alone.


Step 2: Create your project's database and select it
sql
CREATE DATABASE taskflow;
USE taskflow;
CREATE DATABASE makes the folder. USE says "all my next commands apply to this one." You need USE every time you open a new session, or MySQL won't know which database you mean.


Step 3: Create the tasks table
sql
CREATE TABLE tasks (
  id INT AUTO_INCREMENT PRIMARY KEY,
  title VARCHAR(255) NOT NULL,
  created_at TIMESTAMP DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP
);

What each column means:
id INT AUTO_INCREMENT PRIMARY KEY: a whole number that MySQL fills in itself (1, 2, 3...) and that uniquely identifies each row. This replaces our Math.max trick, and unlike it, ids are never reused after a delete.
title VARCHAR(255) NOT NULL: text up to 255 characters, and the row can't be saved without it. This mirrors the title field in your array.
created_at TIMESTAMP DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP: MySQL stamps the time automatically when a row is inserted.

